    overview:
      "Welcome to Swedish contract law. Swedish contract law belongs to the distinct Nordic legal family: unlike Germany, France, or Spain, Sweden never adopted a single comprehensive civil code, and the law instead lives across the 1915 Avtalslagen, the Contracts Act, plus separate statutes for sales and consumer protection. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real Swedish contract dispute actually unfolds. We start with the structural fact that shapes everything else, the absence of a single code, before turning to how a contract actually forms, who can act on another's behalf in forming it, and the formalities the law demands for a small number of specific transactions. We then cover the grounds on which an otherwise validly formed contract can still be invalidated, before turning to what happens once performance goes wrong: the internationally aligned remedy menu Swedish sales law provides, and the sector-specific standard-form contracts that do much of the real risk allocation in Swedish commercial practice. We turn next to the broad judicial power Swedish courts hold to adjust an unreasonable term after the fact, and to the clock that eventually runs out on every claim. We close with the quieter, more distributed way Swedish law achieves the fairness-policing role a single codified good-faith clause plays in France or Germany. Keep one comparison running underneath everything you hear: where a German or French lawyer would reach for one article in a single code, ask which of Sweden's several separate statutes, or which strand of case law, actually does that job instead.",
