          "Historically, a significant share of major international arbitrations involving Russian or former Soviet state parties specified Stockholm or SCC arbitration specifically because of Sweden's Cold War-era reputation for genuine neutrality between East and West, a specific historical niche still shaping Stockholm's arbitration caseload today. A domestic dispute between two Swedish businesses can likewise opt for SCC arbitration under the same institutional framework, trading the Rättegångsbalken's public, court-based process for a private, more flexible one the parties have substantially designed for themselves through their own arbitration agreement.",
